1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,160
You are sitting at your desk.

2
00:00:02,559 --> 00:00:03,980
You have millions of data points

3
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:04,560
at your fingertips.

4
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,680
You've got tables, beautifully colored

5
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,620
dashboards, the latest large language

6
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:12,100
models. An absolute mountain of technology.

7
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:14,640
A mountain. And yet, you still

8
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,520
cannot get a straight, dependable answer

9
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,880
to a painfully simple question like,

10
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,660
which stores need my attention today?

11
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,640
Yeah, it is sort of the

12
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,600
defining paradox of the modern data

13
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,520
stack. We have spent the last

14
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:29,600
two decades focused almost entirely

15
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:30,420
on the plumbing.

16
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:32,740
How to move data faster, how

17
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:34,559
to store more of it cheaper,

18
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:36,200
or how to build better analytical

19
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:38,680
engines. But the data itself.

20
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:40,680
The raw data itself is often

21
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:42,519
just a collection of totally disconnected

22
00:00:42,519 --> 00:00:43,179
facts.
